@node Protocol
@chapter Protocol

@cindex protocol
@cindex UART
The tool and @code{libcgra} talk to the fabric with a byte-oriented UART
protocol (version 3): 115200 baud, 8N1, little-endian payloads.  It is
summarised here for the curious; the authority is @file{hw/rtl/cgra_pkg.vhd},
mirrored in @file{lib/include/cgra.h} and the emulator @file{lib/src/emu.c}.

@cindex commands, protocol
@multitable @columnfractions .12 .12 .50
@headitem Cmd @tab Byte @tab Payload @result{} reply
@item @code{ID}  @tab @code{0x01} @tab --- @result{} @code{0xCA}, version, rows, cols, width
@item @code{CFG} @tab @code{0x02} @tab R*C x 32-bit words + checksum @result{} ACK/NACK
@item @code{WR}  @tab @code{0x03} @tab (R+C) x 16-bit (west rows, then north columns) + checksum @result{} ACK/NACK
@item @code{RUN} @tab @code{0x04} @tab 1 byte step count @result{} ACK
@item @code{RD}  @tab @code{0x05} @tab --- @result{} R*C x 16-bit registers + checksum
@item @code{RST} @tab @code{0x06} @tab --- @result{} ACK (clears registers)
@item @code{EXEC} @tab @code{0x07} @tab steps, flags, 16-bit tap mask, (R+C) x 16-bit inputs + checksum @result{} masked registers + checksum + status
@end multitable

@noindent
@code{ACK} is @code{0x79}, @code{NACK} is @code{0x1F}.  Multi-byte payloads
carry an 8-bit additive checksum (sum of the payload bytes, mod 256); the
device NACKs a bad @code{CFG}/@code{WR} and appends a checksum to the @code{RD}
reply.

Configuration and edge-input words latch before checksum validation.
@code{NACK} does not roll those writes back: resend a complete valid payload
before subsequent execution. A rejected @code{EXEC} leaves the PE registers
unchanged but has latched its input words.

@cindex EXEC
@cindex round trips
@section The fused EXEC transaction
Every kernel drives the array in chunks, and under protocol v2 each chunk cost
three round trips: write the edge inputs, run, read the registers back.
@code{EXEC} (v3) does all three in one, and returns only the registers named by
its 16-bit @dfn{tap mask} --- bit @math{i} selects PE register @math{i},
row-major --- so an element-wise chunk that uses four diagonal lanes ships
eight result bytes instead of thirty-two.  Bit 0 of the flags byte folds in an
@code{RST}. A complete, checksum-valid NACK can then be retried under the
fault model below.

For a valid mask selecting existing PEs, the reply length is fixed, so it
stays parseable even when the payload checksum fails; in that case the device
returns the @emph{current} registers with a @code{NACK} status and does not
step the array.

@code{libcgra} issues @code{EXEC} only when the @code{ID} handshake reports
supported version 3, and otherwise falls back to the @code{WR}/@code{RUN}/@code{RD}
sequence, so an older bitstream keeps working unchanged.

@cindex robustness
@cindex retries
@cindex watchdog
@section Robustness
@code{libcgra} retries complete NACK replies for @code{CFG}, @code{WR} and
@code{EXEC} with fused reset (@code{cgra_set_retries}, default 3). This assumes
intact command bytes and frame lengths. @code{RUN} and @code{RD} are never
retried. Timeout, I/O failure or malformed reply stops the session; later
calls return @code{CGRA_ERR_DESYNC} without sending further bytes. In
particular, the host sends no implicit @code{RST}, which could be interpreted
as payload by a controller still waiting for an argument.

Close the handle and restore the controller parser to idle before reopening:
reset the device out of band, or wait for its configured watchdog and all
pending transmission/replies to finish. Reinitialize configuration, inputs
and datapath before computing again. Reopening alone does not reset the
remote parser; the in-process emulator has no watchdog clock.

The additive checksum and watchdog do not provide reliable framing under byte
insertion/deletion or corrupted command bytes. Some errors are undetectable;
a failed command may already have changed device state. @samp{sim:flaky}
exercises complete NACK retries, while @samp{sim:v2} exercises the legacy
fallback. PTY regressions check session shutdown under timeout and malformed
replies. These tests do not establish recovery from arbitrary link faults.

@code{ID} dimensions determine transfer lengths and row-major PE indices
@code{r*C+c}. Supported devices have positive R/C and at most 16 PEs. The EXEC
mask always occupies two bytes: the device ignores bits beyond R*C and the
host rejects such masks. Only selected existing registers are returned.
